WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:22.920
When we need to study something in science
that is too large or complex to easily work

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:29.360
with, we’ll often make a model of it in
order to recreate it in a simpler way.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.930
You might not realize it, but we actually
use models all the time in our everyday lives.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:41.910
They can be static and representational, like
the New York City subway map – or they can

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:48.890
be used to make predictions of dynamic systems,
like a weather forecast or the ups-and-downs

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:54.160
of the stock market.
Even our understanding of climate change,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:02.050
a phenomenon on the tip of everyone’s tongues,
is based on predictions made by climate models.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:10.369
Global Climate Models, otherwise known as
GCMs, are gigantic computer codes of mathematical

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:16.100
representations of our Earth’s climate system,
and they are generated based on the laws of

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.970
physics.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:22.189
Let’s take a look at how it works.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:28.799
Energy enters the atmosphere as solar radiation,
which then transfers and circulates within

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:36.149
the earth's system. To track how the energy
flows through this system, GCMs try to simulate

00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:42.359
the earth’s climate conditions. They account
for processes such as solar inputs, the heating

00:01:42.359 --> 00:01:47.209
and reflection from the land and water, the
movement of air masses,

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:53.609
and ice-albedo response.
GCMs divide the earth into thousands of gridded

00:01:53.609 --> 00:02:02.009
3-D cells. Each grid box holds a single value
of the atmospheric variable such as winds,

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:10.780
temperature, pressure, humidity, cloud cover
and cloud ice that is being predicted. In

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:16.610
the past thirty years, due to advancement
in computational power, the size of the gridded

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:23.400
boxes has been reduced, and the mathematical
equations are more accurately solved.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:30.870
As the earth is divided into discrete cells,
so too is time divided into finite intervals,

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:37.500
or time-steps. If we want to compute quickly
the global climate in 10 years, we might be

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.690
tempted to do it in one go, with a time-step
of 10 years. Such a large time-step is impossible

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:51.780
though, because the energy we are accounting
for within a cell would have moved on into

00:02:51.780 --> 00:03:00.130
adjacent cells. Movement of air, for example,
is a factor that may only need a small time-step

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:08.210
of 30 minutes or less. So instead, GCMs take
an initial climate state and calculate the

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:15.510
climate state one time-step away, after which
they repeat the calculation with the resulting

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:22.190
new climate state. This is repeated until
the total number of time-steps reaches our

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:27.650
desired target time of 10 years.
It might seem like there would be lots of

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:34.020
room for error in so much processing, but
think of it this way. When we flip a coin,

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:42.370
it is hard to predict whether the result will
be heads or tails. However, over many flips,

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:51.540
we can reliably say that the probability of
a heads is 50%. GCMs have time-scales that

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:59.240
are decades long, which means hundreds of
thousands of time-steps, or coin tosses. Because

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:06.820
GCMs simulate the earth’s climate over large
time-scales, many consider GCMs to be robust

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:10.680
in comparison to shorter time-scale models.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:18.720
To test the GCMs, scientists have gone back
and used the models to recreate climate history.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:25.160
This way, they can compare the models’ predictions
with actual empirical evidence recorded in

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.280
the past.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:34.650
They started at 1900, and attempted to predict
the climate changes over the next 100+ years

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:41.630
– a century in which an unprecedented spike
in temperature occurred.

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:49.460
They ran the model twice: the first time,
they only included natural forces. The second

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:56.020
time, they included human factors in the equations,
accounting for the increased addition of greenhouse

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.840
gases in the atmosphere .

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:05.430
Comparing the observed global and regional
climate data from 100+ years, the first round

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:13.630
of GCMs predictions didn’t match up. But
when the models ran the second time, accounting

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:22.020
for both natural and human factors affecting
climate, the results were incredible. Not

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:28.660
only do the predictions lie within a 95% confidence
interval of the actual data, but they are

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:36.120
correct for both the continents and the oceans
individually. And it wasn’t only one GCM

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:42.780
that found this to be true – the results
of every single one of the 15 independently

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:50.300
run GCMs agreed.
This allows scientists to attribute the climate

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:58.400
changes they have observed to human factors
such as increased industrialization, deforestation

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:05.430
and urbanizations which lead to an increase
in the concentration of greenhouse gases.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:11.190
If you’re still skeptical, let’s take
a look at another example.

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:17.780
When the first sophisticated GCMs were developed
and run in the 1990s, the predictions they

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:23.590
generated didn’t seem to make any sense.
They indicated that there would be a rapid

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:29.490
heating of the upper layers of the troposphere
and a corresponding cooling of the lower levels

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:36.270
of the stratosphere.
But when the recorded data from 1960 onwards

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:44.820
was examined, it turned out that the GCM’s
predictions were verified. The cause was,

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:52.300
once again, the effect of greenhouse gases.
These gases, concentrated in the troposphere,

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:59.150
were trapping low frequency radiation -- causing
the unexpected warming of the troposphere

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:05.460
and the corresponding cooling of the stratosphere.
So even though not all models make correct

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:13.100
predictions every single time, the GCMs have
proven over and over again to be reliable

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:20.870
and sophisticated systems. And if the past
is any indication of the future, we would

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:24.130
be wise to heed its predictions.

